Subject: [PATCH] DRM: poll all mouse-classified evdev devices, not just the
 last match

ConfigureEvdevDevice()'s mouse-vs-touch priority logic had this rule
for two non-touch mouse devices: "Current is mouse, new is mouse? Use
the new one" - meaning each newly found mouse-classified device closed
and overwrote the previous one, so whichever mouse-like device was
enumerated LAST always won, regardless of whether it actually produced
real movement/click events. Confirmed on real hardware: a physical USB
mouse not working at all on one machine, while working fine on
another - consistent with readdir() enumeration order (not guaranteed
stable/predictable) happening to put a non-functional device last on
the affected machine.

Same underlying pattern as the existing 0001 multi-keyboard-fd patch,
applied to mice instead: turns platform.mouseFd into an array
(mouseFds[]/mouseFdCount) for non-touch mouse devices specifically,
and polls all of them in PollMouseEvents() instead of only the last
one found.

Touch devices are deliberately NOT included in this change - they keep
the original single-fd/"last one wins" behaviour via platform.mouseFd,
and their existing priority over regular mice (touch always wins if
both are present, in either enumeration order) is preserved exactly as
before - only the specific "which one of possibly several actual mouse
devices gets used" decision changes.

Verified: the touch-vs-mouse priority/exclusivity decision logic
tested in isolation against 4 scenarios (multiple mice all accepted
and polled; touch arriving after mice correctly discards the mice;
mouse arriving after touch correctly gets ignored; multiple touch
devices still follow the original last-one-wins behaviour) - all
passing. Full file (with 0001 and 0002 already applied) compiles
cleanly, and the app links and starts without crashing against the
resulting library.

Upstream-Status: Pending

